<title>AUSTRALIA: RTRS-BHP says 11-month steel sales rise.</title>
<headline>RTRS-BHP says 11-month steel sales rise.</headline>
<dateline>MELBOURNE 1997-05-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Broken Hill Proprietary Co Ltd, Australia's largest steelmaker, said on Monday that its steel despatches in the first 11 months of 1996/97 rose by three percent to 7.577 million tonnes.</p>
<p>However, the company said its raw steelmake over the same period to April 30 was two percent lower at 7.759 million tonnes.  </p>
<p>BHP, which recently announced plans to close its Newcastle steelworks in 1999 and to keep its Port Kembla and Whyalla plants open, said steel despatches in April rose by 15 percent to 749,000 tonnes from a year-ago and the monthly steelmake rose by eight percent to 753,000 tonnes.</p>
<p>It said the higher monthly steel output was due to good operations in both iron-making and steelmaking at Port Kembla, located south of Sydney in the state of New South Wales, and continued high output following the Number 4 blast furnace reline at Newcastle, north of Sydney. It said steel despatches from Delta, Ohio, had been included for the first time in April.  </p>
